to </c><00:00:01.899><c>hear  </c><00:00:01.900><c>your </c><00:00:01.901><c>best </c><00:00:01.902><c>advice </c><00:00:01.903><c>for </c><00:00:01.904><c>how </c><00:00:01.905><c>to </c><00:00:01.906><c>play  </c><00:00:01.907><c>let </c><00:00:01.908><c>us </c><00:00:01.909><c>know </c><00:00:01.910><c>about </c><00:00:01.911><c>your </c><00:00:01.912><c>favorite </c><00:00:01.913><c>tactics  </c><00:00:01.914><c>in </c><00:00:01.915><c>the </c><00:00:01.916><c>comments </c><00:00:01.917><c>below </c><00:00:01.918><c>and </c><00:00:01.919><c>check </c><00:00:01.920><c>out </c><00:00:01.921><c>our  </c><00:00:01.922><c>other </c><00:00:01.923><c>coverage </c><00:00:01.924><c>of </c><00:00:01.925><c>xcom </c><00:00:01.926><c>chimera </c><00:00:01.927><c>squad  </c><00:00:01.928><c>thanks </c><00:00:01.929><c>for </c><00:00:01.930><c>watching  </c><00:00:01.931><c>[Music]  </c><00:00:01.932><c>you</c>

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:04.789 align:start position:0%
xcom is a fantastic turn-based strategy  series and the standalone spin-off  chimera squad makes some big changes to  tactics to help you avoid the kind of  mistakes that will end your first play  through early  we're going to get you caught up on  x-com chimera squad's new mechanics  number one breach breaching is one of  chimaera squad's most enjoyable  additions to xcom  in previous games you always had the  option to rip open a wall and make your  own fun but now it's mandatory  each mission has a number of set  encounters typically one to three and  each encounter starts with a breach  this seems to be an interesting solution  to cutting down on running in overwatch  between  fights the absolute most important thing  to understand about breaching is that  the order you stack up on the door  determines both the order the agents  enter the room and also where they take  cover afterwards you can change the  breach order with these small arrows up  top  they're kind of easy to miss you cannot  however control precisely where agents  take cover so  plan carefully that being said don't be  afraid to restart if things aren't  looking too good  the order of your agent's breach is also  critical because they have breach  specific abilities  for example who you levitate who you  blind and who you spray acid on can make  a big difference  agents can also have breaching gear like  flashbangs and smoke  in order to use it you'll need to stack  them up on the door in a way that makes  sense  for example the door kicker can't also  throw a grenade breaching gear can be  found in this little area  also easy to miss some great tips for  breaching are  don't under commit to a breach point  since you don't know what's behind the  door  you don't want an agent in a room alone  there are usually multiple breach points  with different bonuses and penalties to  consider  which agent you assign matters so for  example make sure to send a tanky  character through a door  that roots them in place for a turn know  what the labels mean over enemies  these are aggressive alert and surprised  aggressive enemies will fire back in the  breaching phase and need to be dealt  with first then you can probably focus  on enemies you know will be a problem  down the line  if the room doesn't have aggressive  enemies you can avoid wasting defensive  breaching actions  that pull aggro at the expense of  shooting pay attention to how many  encounters you're about to do on a  mission  and conserve your resources for later  rooms if possible some abilities can  only be used once a mission  if there's only one encounter then  you're good light them up  finally like previous games you can  still make your own holes after breach  phase by tearing through walls with  explosives  and it's still a hell of a lot of fun  number two turn order there's a new  order listed on the right side of the  screen that mixes up good guy and bad  guy turns  so consider making your decisions not  based on who's the closest  or who has the most dangerous weapon but  on who's going next  in other words the person with the gun  next to you can be less dangerous than  the person at the back of the room who's  about to fire  you can also reference the turn order  which is listed over each enemy's head  each agent technically gets two actions  but certain equipment and gear doesn't  count against it just make sure to do  everything you want before firing  because that action almost always ends  the turn until you buy certain mods  later  early in the game it's good to just be  aggressive and go after the next enemy  in line but later on you should learn to  manipulate the turn order in your favor  for example there's a team-up mechanic  that lets you bring up one agent in the  order once permission  there are also team abilities that allow  you to swap places with fellow agents or  delay an opponent's action  some agents can also constrict enemies  and negate their turn  some can give away turns or lock down  enemies with psionic abilities  if you're in a good spot you can also  use preparation a type of hunker down  mechanic that increases your defensive  rating  gets rid of burning and also gives you  another turn in fairly short order  before the end of the round  when you get the hang of turn order you  can do some really fun things like  teleport an enemy to your team and give  them a serious beatdown  speaking of beatdowns number three make  arrests  in chimera squad you are a paramilitary  police force which means  killing isn't the only or best option  you can and should make plenty of  arrests because they net you valuable  intel that you can then use to cut down  on  unrest unrest is an ever-present thing  you need to manage and failing to do so  can lose the game  more on that in a bit you can also trade  in intel on the black market for certain  items like weapon mods the arrest action  also appears to let you travel the full  distance of a dash and then attack  basically free run and gun  another caller a great technique to make  arrests  is to attempt to wound as many enemies  as possible during the breach phase and  then mop up the room with fisticuffs  there are specialized tools you can buy  later on such as tranquilizer rounds to  make arrests with less risk  arrests can also be a very satisfying  way to end an encounter  just don't try to knock out enemies in a  bad situation or  it might get you killed number four  death  if you're an xcom veteran you already  know that xcom can be punishing at  almost every difficulty  in chimera losing an agent fails the  game automatically and you'll have to  restart the encounter  agents die when they lose all of their  health and are not stabilized by a  teammate within a few turns  there are things you can do to lower  your chances of losing though such as  building android replacements  androids are squad replacements that  show up between breaches when an agent  is incapacitated they can be a bit  pricey but they come in clutch  if androids are damaged they can be  repaired back at base but if they're  destroyed  they are dead there is still technically  permadeath if you consider androids to  be alive  the other big thing you'll have to adapt  your mindset to is how gravely wounded  agents work differently  unlike previous xcom games they aren't  out of action for long periods of time  instead they develop negative traits in  the form of scars scars can cause  problems like greatly restrict movements  reduce your ability to land shots or  cause agents to panic  some of these scars can be a real  problem and it's in your interest to  either dedicate some time for those  agents at a training center to remove  the scars or put them on desk duty for a  while in the form of research projects  or spec ops missions which will net you  extra resources  number five unrest city 31 has an unrest  mechanic  similar to many previous xcom games if  you lose enough districts to unrest  you'll lose the city  and like other x-coms this can happen  long before you even know it so save  periodically  each district can have a field team  attached to it which you purchase with  intel  remember that bit about making arrests  earlier field teams have the ability to  reduce or even freeze the buildup of  unrest and can be upgraded to do even  more  do not neglect your field teams number  six  tweak early research priorities like  previous xcoms if you go into a mission  under equipped you can get yourself into  an unwinnable situation  unlike earlier xcom games you can  slightly tweak your priorities because  death and wounds work differently  agents with scars aren't fun but they  can be managed so here's a few  recommendations  you can probably put off armor research  a bit longer than you're comfortable  with to invest in android replacements  early armor research honestly doesn't  give you that much more protection  versus having a full squad member ready  to go as backup since androids do have  permadeath and are expensive to replace  take some time to boost their  survivability invest in breaching items  because they're really just a hell of a  lot of fun  entering a room protected by smoke or  with stunned enemies can be the key to  seizing early advantages  research and buy throwables that can  mess with the enemy team until you  develop agents to manipulate the turn  order  turn order is king i'm sure you have a  lot of thoughts about these changes to  the xcom formula and we want to hear  your best advice for how to play  let us know about your favorite tactics  in the comments below and check out our  other coverage of xcom chimera squad  thanks for watching  [Music]  you
 

